# Operations-Analyst Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Author role:** Operations analyst — throughput, delay, coordination, and operator-workload lens.
**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Independence note:** Written without reading any other Stage 1 brief. Uses `outputs/context/airport-context.md` as factual scaffolding only.

---

## Executive summary (operational thesis)

A quiet-by-design standard is defensible as an operations standard, not as an ambience standard. The four peer airports MWAA is likely to be measured against — Amsterdam Schiphol, Helsinki-Vantaa, Copenhagen Kastrup, and London City — did not "eliminate announcements." Each of them replaced a broadcast-everywhere paging model with a bounded operating model: announcements permitted for boarding calls at the gate, safety, and irregular operations, and suppressed everywhere else in the terminal envelope. Helsinki has run this model since 2015 [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. London City has run it since 2008 [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. The critical operational fact is that these are policies about *where and when* audio is used, coupled with *measured* acoustic and intelligibility performance where it is used. That distinction — governance plus commissioning, not a moratorium on paging — is the standard MWAA can actually enforce.

The operational risk is not that MWAA will make the terminal too quiet. It is that MWAA will adopt "silent airport" branding without the acoustic performance floor, the PA zoning, the IROP override protocol, and the accessibility redundancy that make the model safe. The peer literature and the ACRP evidence base support only one workable formulation: define ambient sound, reverberation, and Speech Transmission Index (STI) as measured performance requirements; specify the zones in which paging is permitted and the zones in which it is suppressed; and pre-approve an IROP override that returns to broadcast paging when the operating environment demands it. Everything else is decoration.

Two conditions at IAD and DCA change what "the same standard" means at each airport. IAD served 29.0M passengers in 2025, an all-time record, and is entering a $20B+ decade of terminal construction — a greenfield problem [Source: https://www.ffxnow.com/2025/03/03/dulles-airports-2024-passenger-total-set-new-all-time-record/]. DCA served 24.9M in 2025, down 5.3% year-over-year, operates in a historically constrained footprint, and absorbed both the January 2025 mid-air collision aftermath and the FAA's now-permanent helicopter restrictions effective January 23, 2026 — a brownfield problem in a terminal envelope that cannot easily be re-zoned acoustically [Source: https://simpleflying.com/faa-makes-dc-helicopter-ban-permanent-new-flight-rules-explained/]. A single standard is defensible only if its mandatory tier is written as *performance*, not *product* or *architecture*.

