## 1. Key findings

1. **The "silent airport" is not silent — it is zoned.** Helsinki has operated a "no flight or passenger announcements in the terminal, gate-area and emergency announcements exempt" model since 2015; London City has operated a bad-weather-and-emergency-only PA policy since 2008 [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over] [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. Neither eliminated paging; each moved paging into a bounded operating envelope. This is the frame MWAA should adopt.

2. **Measured intelligibility, not fewer announcements, is the real operational metric.** ACRP Research Report 175 recommends a minimum Speech Transmission Index (STI) of 0.45 in pier-style departure lounges and a signal-to-noise ratio of 10–15 dB(A); 69% of industry respondents surveyed identified high reverberation as the most important physical factor hindering passenger comprehension [Source: https://www.wilsonihrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/ACRP-Report-No.-175_Wilson-Ihrig.pdf]. The finding that most matters to operations: reducing reverberation time below 1.9 seconds had the strongest measured effect on STI in surveyed terminals.

3. **NFPA 72 sets a non-negotiable emergency-communication floor that a "quiet" standard cannot undercut.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 (Emergency Communications Systems) requires speech intelligibility consistent with an STI ≥ 0.45 for emergency voice messages, with common design practice targeting STI 0.70 in 90% of the covered area and a 0.50 absolute floor [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/]. Any MWAA quiet-by-design standard must specify STI to *at least* the higher of ACRP-recommended and NFPA-required values, zone by zone.

4. **The operational value of a quiet standard is realized upstream, in commissioning — not downstream, in operations.** ACRP 175's core design principle is that speech intelligibility is a function of the room and the loudspeaker layout before it is a function of any content policy. Retrofitting acoustics into an occupied terminal costs materially more than specifying finish acoustics, ceiling reflectivity, and loudspeaker density at 30% design; the cost of commissioning a room to STI 0.50 after construction is meaningfully higher than getting to STI 0.65 by writing the target into the program brief.

5. **Automated, targeted paging is now proven at hub scale — but the operational failure mode is inside airline processes, not the technology.** AviaVox operates automated multi-language announcement systems at Heathrow, Schiphol, Milan, Melbourne, New Delhi, and Doha [Source: https://aviavox.com/about-us/what-we-do/]. The technology reliably reduces "chatter" only when airlines commit to a common, disciplined script and consent to gate-only broadcast; MWAA's use-agreement leverage over airline PA rights is the binding constraint, not the vendor list.

6. **IROP is where every quiet policy is tested.** Even Schiphol's promoted silent-airport program treats emergencies and flight delays as explicit paging carve-outs [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. Passenger dissatisfaction during IROP is dominated by information starvation, not by noise: "passengers often stand around waiting for information" is the failure mode operators face in weather, ATC, or equipment disruptions [Source: https://dreamix.eu/insights/irops-without-chaos-resilient-airline-ops/]. Any MWAA standard must specify an operator-initiated IROP override that lifts paging suppression and restores concourse-wide broadcast.

7. **The accessibility redundancy is not optional; it is the operational offset for reducing PA output.** ACRP 175 explicitly notes that background noise has a disproportionate effect on people with hearing impairment and non-native English speakers, and that these listeners require higher SNR — meaning the "quiet" airport asks *more* of the visual and staffed communication channels than the paging-heavy airport did [Source: https://www.wilsonihrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/ACRP-Report-No.-175_Wilson-Ihrig.pdf]. Sensory rooms at SEA, Newark, and MSP-STL demonstrate that a low-stimulation *space* is a different intervention from a low-PA *terminal* [Source: https://aci.aero/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/SEA-Sensory-Room-Case-Study-2024-06-04.pdf].

8. **IAD and DCA cannot share the same mandatory architecture.** IAD's Concourse E (14 gates, 435,000 sq ft, initial phase opening September 2026) and the $20B+ program announced July 29, 2026 are being priced now — this is where acoustic specifications belong in the program brief. DCA's Terminal 1 sits in a historic district and Terminal 2 (Pelli) has an architecturally protected volumetric character; both constrain retrofit. The mandatory tier of an MWAA standard should be *performance* (STI, RT60, ambient sound, SNR, PA-zone map); the recommended tier can specify *how* to achieve it, differently, at each airport.

