## 2. Evidence — the operating models that actually exist

### 2.1 Helsinki-Vantaa — the reference case

Helsinki has been operated as a "silent airport" since 2015 under a formally structured Silent Airport project run by Finavia in collaboration with the airlines. The operating model has three pieces: (1) no flight or passenger announcements in the terminal envelope; (2) gate-area boarding announcements exempt; (3) emergency announcements exempt. Passenger-side offsets include banners under FIDS advising travelers to track their own time and use the airport app for flight status [Source: https://airport.nridigital.com/air_mar18/shhhh_is_the_day_of_the_airport_announcer_over]. What is publicly documented is the policy; what is not publicly documented in the sources reviewed is the measured effect on missed boardings.

**Operational read.** The Helsinki design is the cleanest one for MWAA to study because it is an airline-airport joint operating agreement, not a marketing overlay. The instrument matters. Getting United (IAD) and American (DCA) to sign a similar disciplined-paging protocol is the same class of negotiation as the 2025 use-and-lease agreement, and it belongs in the same instrument, not in a design manual alone.

### 2.2 London City — the smallest, tightest case

London City has run a silent terminal policy since 2008. Paging is permitted only for weather-related delays and genuine emergencies; boarding calls are made only at the gate; all other passenger communication is delivered on FIDS, staffed positions, and mobile app [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/silent-airport-policy]. Passenger reception is documented as initially disruptive and then positive as the model becomes familiar. This case is instructive because London City is a small, single-terminal airport with strong business-traveler dominance — the demographic most likely to succeed under a self-service information regime, and the least representative of DCA's leisure-plus-government mix or IAD's high international/first-time-traveler share.

**Operational read.** London City is the model for a *departure lounge* at IAD or DCA; it is not the model for the *concourse crossings, curb-to-gate transitions, or CBP hall* where first-time and international passengers concentrate.

### 2.3 Amsterdam Schiphol — the case with automated tooling

Schiphol operates AviaVox-driven automated multilingual announcement systems, with paging concentrated in gate zones and largely suppressed in circulation space. Observers report walking from curb to gate without hearing a PA announcement [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/silence-at-amsterdam-airport-schiphol/]. The technology deployment is meaningful: AviaVox's operational claim is high-intelligibility, multi-language automated calls that are indistinguishable from human speech and that can be targeted zone-by-zone [Source: https://aviavox.com/about-us/what-we-do/]. Independent measured passenger outcomes attributable to the announcement policy specifically (isolated from the many other quality investments Schiphol has made) were not located in the sources reviewed; this is an evidence gap.

**Operational read.** MWAA should treat automated-paging technology as the *enabling* layer, not the *governing* layer. Buying the vendor without writing the script discipline and the zone map is procurement without a policy — and it is how "silent airport" branding decays into a paging-heavy terminal with a fancier voice.

### 2.4 Copenhagen — the "quiet space" model

Copenhagen has designated quiet zones (airside, 2nd floor, C gates; silent lounge at A4) rather than a whole-terminal silent policy [Source: https://www.sleepinginairports.net/guides/copenhagen-kastrup-airport-guide.htm]. This is the *localized* version of the intervention: acoustic and PA discipline in specific bounded rooms rather than across the concourse. This is the cheapest and lowest-risk lever available to MWAA at DCA in the near term — a defined quiet room or two per concourse is a solved architectural problem.

**Operational read.** For DCA, the Copenhagen model is a credible interim standard while the mandatory MWAA-wide requirements are commissioned for future IAD terminals.

### 2.5 What ACRP 175 actually says

The ACRP research base — the closest thing to a canonical US-airport study of PA intelligibility — establishes the performance floor MWAA should be writing to:

- STI ≥ 0.45 minimum for public address broadcasts in pier-style departure lounges [Source: https://www.wilsonihrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/ACRP-Report-No.-175_Wilson-Ihrig.pdf].
- Signal-to-noise ratio 10–15 dB(A) target [Source: same].
- Reverberation time below 1.9 seconds is the strongest single lever for increasing STI [Source: same].
- 69% of industry survey respondents cite reverberation as the most important physical factor hindering PA comprehension [Source: same].
- Background noise disproportionately harms hearing-impaired and non-native-English listeners — the accessibility populations MWAA is legally obligated to serve.

The MWAA standard should not invent numbers. It should adopt ACRP-recommended values as its mandatory floor for revenue-service passenger zones and NFPA 72 Chapter 24 emergency-messaging STI as its mandatory floor everywhere audio can be used to instruct occupants.

### 2.6 NFPA 72 is the load-bearing regulatory constraint

Emergency communications systems fall under NFPA 72 Chapter 24, adopted by reference through the Virginia Uniform Statewide Building Code and the DC Construction Codes. The intelligibility requirement is expressed via CIS/STI: STI 0.45 (CIS "Fair") is the code-cited floor for acceptable emergency messaging, with best-practice design targeting STI 0.70 in 90% of the covered area and treating STI 0.50 as an absolute minimum [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/]. The operational implication: a quiet-by-design standard cannot lower loudspeaker density, defer commissioning, or exempt zones in ways that make the emergency-communication STI unachievable. Every PA zoning decision has an emergency-messaging shadow, and the standard must state that explicitly.

### 2.7 The IROP failure mode

The literature on irregular operations is unanimous that the dominant driver of passenger dissatisfaction under disruption is information starvation, not noise [Source: https://dreamix.eu/insights/irops-without-chaos-resilient-airline-ops/]. Every documented silent-airport policy carves out flight delays and emergencies as explicit paging exemptions. MWAA's standard should therefore include, as a mandatory operational element: (a) a defined operator role empowered to invoke an IROP paging override; (b) a pre-scripted set of paging content categories that revert to concourse-wide broadcast during override; and (c) a test protocol run under exercise conditions before the override is ever needed live. Without this, the quiet standard becomes brittle: the first weather event that overwhelms staffed and digital channels will validate every skeptic.

### 2.8 Operating environment at IAD and DCA

- IAD served 29,008,839 passengers in 2025, a 6.4% year-over-year increase and an all-time record [Source: https://www.americanairportguide.com/iad/airport-statistics.htm]. On-time arrivals ran 77.38% in November 2025 [Source: same]. Concourse E's first phase opens September 2026 and is the first live test bed available for any acoustic and PA standard MWAA adopts.
- DCA served ~24.9M passengers in 2025, down 5.3%, absorbing the January 29, 2025 mid-air collision aftermath and the permanent FAA helicopter restrictions that took effect January 23, 2026 [Source: https://simpleflying.com/faa-makes-dc-helicopter-ban-permanent-new-flight-rules-explained/].
- The 15-year airline use-and-lease agreement effective January 1, 2025 is the commercial instrument through which airline-controlled gate paging can be disciplined; without engaging that instrument, any MWAA standard governs only public-area PA and leaves the loudest surface — the airline gate microphone — untouched.

