## 3. Counterexamples — when operations cannot solve what only capital can

Three cases where the operational lever is insufficient and the answer is physical or programmatic capital:

**a. Rooms with RT60 above ~2.0 seconds cannot be made intelligible with content policy alone.** Terminals with hard finishes, high ceilings, curtain walls, and no acoustic ceiling absorption sit in an intelligibility regime where no reasonable adjustment to announcement policy, script length, or scheduling can bring STI to 0.45 [Source: https://www.wilsonihrig.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/ACRP-Report-No.-175_Wilson-Ihrig.pdf]. DCA Terminal 1 (Bolling) and the historically constrained Pelli volumes at Terminal 2 face this constraint in specific rooms. In those rooms, the standard's mandatory intelligibility target *requires* physical acoustic treatment; there is no operational shortcut.

**b. Emergency communication cannot be achieved through visual and mobile channels alone.** NFPA 72 Chapter 24 exists because the code authorities have determined that visual-only mass notification is not an acceptable substitute for intelligible voice notification for evacuation and shelter-in-place instructions. MWAA cannot delete loudspeakers from a zone on quiet-airport grounds; it can only re-engineer their density, directionality, and processing to remain code-compliant.

**c. Accessibility redundancy has a physical footprint.** Replacing "the announcement told me" with FIDS, digital signage, mobile alerts, and staffed information positions requires screen counts, staffing positions, sensory rooms, and BLE beacon infrastructure that do not appear in a program brief unless the standard requires them. This is where the quiet standard *creates* capital demand rather than avoiding it — an honest analysis acknowledges the offset.

