## 6. Recommended operational shape of the standard (for downstream synthesis)

Not a full recommendation — the operations lens; other lenses will refine.

- **Mandatory tier (both airports, all new and substantially renovated space):**
  - STI ≥ 0.50 at 90% of measured receiver locations in all zones where PA is used, per NFPA 72 Ch. 24 practice.
  - STI ≥ 0.45 minimum floor per ACRP 175 for passenger-area PA.
  - RT60 ≤ 1.5 s in departure lounges; ≤ 1.9 s in circulation zones, per ACRP 175 directional guidance.
  - Signal-to-noise ratio 10–15 dB(A) target for PA content.
  - PA zoning map required in every design submittal, at 30% design, identifying paging zones and non-paging zones.
  - Pre-scripted IROP paging override protocol with an owner, an activation criterion, and a quarterly exercise requirement.
  - Redundant visual, digital, and staffed communication capability for every content category that PA no longer delivers by default.
- **Recommended tier (achievement pathways):**
  - Automated multi-language PA content generation (AviaVox or equivalent) for scheduled paging.
  - Bounded quiet zones on the Copenhagen model where the geometry permits.
- **Context-dependent tier (differs at IAD vs DCA):**
  - IAD greenfield concourses (E, future Tier 2/3): full performance floor plus zoning at design, commissioned before revenue service.
  - DCA existing terminals: performance floor applied to substantial renovations only; quiet-zone strategy on the Copenhagen model as an interim measure in unaltered rooms; historic-district constraints acknowledged as documented exceptions with a compensating operational plan.

The standard is defensible only if it is written as measured performance the airport can verify, not as aspiration the airport can only claim.
